<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wanyan Xiyin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1140</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wanyan Xiyin was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> noble and minister of the Jin dynasty, a kinsman of its founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-jin">Aguda</a>. At Aguda's command he created the Jurchen script in 1119, combining elements of Chinese characters and the Khitan scripts, which was used for government documents and translations of Chinese classics. He also commanded in the wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, and was executed during a court struggle in 1140.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> name is transcribed in Chinese sources as Gushen, Wushi or Hushe; Wanyan Xiyin is his sinicised name. He belonged to the Wanyan tribe, the ruling clan of the Jin dynasty, but not to the direct imperial line: his father Huandu was a cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-jin">Aguda</a> (Emperor Taizu). Modern writers describe him as the chief shaman of the pre-Jin Jurchen state. A contemporary Chinese source says he devised the laws and script of the Jurchen and so shaped them into one state, and that his people called him shan-man, shaman, because he understood changing conditions like a god.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>With Nianhan (Wanyan Zonghan) he was one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-jin">Aguda</a>'s chief advisers, helping him to unite the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> tribes under the Wanyan tribe's leadership and to overthrow the Khitan-led Liao dynasty. On Aguda's orders he created the Jurchen large-character script, based on Chinese characters and the Khitan script, for the administration of the new empire. After Aguda's death he served as a civil minister at the court of Wuqimai (Emperor Taizong), Aguda's younger brother and successor.</p><p>Wanyan Xiyin was fascinated by the Chinese classics and gathered a large library when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> looted Bianjing, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Northern Song</a> capital, in the Jin–Song Wars. He invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> scholars led by Yuwen Xuzhong, a Song envoy held by the Jurchens, to advise him and teach his sons and grandsons. Hong Hao, another detained Song envoy, thought that forms of government organisation, official ranks, salaries, posthumous names for emperors and the taboo on characters in emperors' names entered the Jin through Yuwen Xuzhong. Xiyin's sons were able to write Chinese poems for Hong Hao.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He fell into disgrace under Emperor Xizong and died in 1140, the same year as his three sons, Wanyan Bada, Wanyan Mandai and Wanyan Tata. The tombs of Wanyan Xiyin and his family are said to lie near Shulan in Jilin Province, and since 1961 they have been on the provincial register of protected historical sites.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wanyan Xiyin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A Jurchen script, based partly on Chinese characters and the Khitan scripts, had been created in 1119 by <b>Wanyan Xiyin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The early Jurchen script was invented in 1120 by <b>Wanyan Xiyin</b> on the orders of Wanyan Aguda.</blockquote><a href="/culture/jurchens">Jurchens · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
